<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Penn Museum</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1887">1887</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as University of Pennsylvania Museum of Archaeology and Anthropology</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Penn Museum, formally the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a> Museum of Archaeology and Anthropology, is a museum and research institution in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a> founded in 1887. It has sponsored excavations across the world, including at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a>, at Hasanlu in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and at sites in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a>. Its collections number around a million objects.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/penn-museum.jpg" alt="Penn Museum" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ajay_suresh · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Penn_Museum_-_UPenn_(53590175396).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…region, was first dug for six weeks by Robert Koldewey in 1887, who took it for an extended burial place; Thorkild Jacobsen and Fuad Safar found the first evidence of its identification as Lagash in 1953, and excavation resumed in 2019 under Holly Pittman of the <b>Penn Museum</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lagash">Lagash · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The treasures are now divided between the British Museum, the <b>Penn Museum</b> and the Baghdad Museum, and the partly restored ziggurat, built by Ur-Nammu and repaired by Ishme-Dagan of Isin, Kurigalzu I and Nabonidus, remains the best-preserved landmark of the site.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ur">Ur · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Penn Museum</b> excavations under Robert Dyson (1956 to 1977) found hundreds of bodies, including the pair known as the 'Hasanlu Lovers'.</blockquote><a href="/state/hasanlu">Hasanlu</a></li></ul></section>
